@using BrainHarbor.Web.Models
@inject BrainHarbor.Web.Feed.CardImages CardImages
@model FeedCard

@*
    One feed card (WI-109), identical on the homepage and /research.

    Anatomy: hero (content-matched photo with the journey path laid OVER it) →
    title → hook → tags + date · source.

    Two handoffs have now shaped this card, and the history matters because it
    reads like a reversal and isn't quite one:

    - 2026-08-15: the homepage handoff wanted a plain card, no photo and no
      dial. Dan kept both, because "how far along the ready scale" was
      information the homepage lacked and the badge did not carry.
    - 2026-08-19: the journey handoff replaced the 0-10 dial AND the badge with
      a single four-stage path. Dan's call: keep the photos, drop the dial, and
      put the path where the dial was — ON the image, not under it. The path
      answers the question the dial was there for, in words a patient can
      repeat, without a number that reads as a percentage of a cure.

    So the photo survived both rounds and the dial did not, but the dial's
    POSITION survived: it was the most prominent thing on the card and the
    indicator that replaced it had to inherit that prominence, not be demoted
    to a row of dots beneath the picture. See docs/design/README.md.
*@
<li class="card">
    @* The path sits inside the hero, so it needs its own opaque surface: a
       photo backdrop varies per card and cannot be relied on for contrast.
       The dark plate does what the dial's dark disc did — guarantees the
       measured contrast ratios no matter which image is behind it. *@
    <div class="card-hero" style="@BrainHarbor.Web.Feed.CardArt.HeroStyle(CardImages.UrlFor(Model))">
        <span class="card-hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="card-hero__indicator">
            <partial name="_JourneyPath" model="Model.Stage"
                     view-data='new ViewDataDictionary(ViewData) { ["Modifier"] = "journey--over" }' />
        </div>
    </div>
    <h3><a href="@Model.Url">@Model.Title</a></h3>
    <p class="card__hook">@Model.Hook</p>
    <p class="card__meta">
        @* Topic chips stay: tumor type is how a patient finds the item that is
           actually about them. *@
        @foreach (var tag in Model.Tags)
        {
            <span class="tag">@tag</span>
        }
        <span>@Model.DateText · @Model.Source</span>
    </p>
</li>
